      {
        "name": "Reconciliation",
        "cells": {
          "A1": {
            "value": "Analytical Commentary & Recommendation",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A2": {
            "value": "Convergence Analysis",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A3": {
            "value": "DCF base case (₹1,382/share) and global mature peer comps (20x EV/EBITDA, ~₹1,400/share) converge tightly, anchoring fair value via two independent methodologies across a terminal-state lens. Indian peer comps (28x EV/EBITDA, ~₹2,400/share) reflect current-phase sector pricing during a high-growth, premium-valuation window. Current market price (₹2,920/share) trades above all three core methodology anchors, implying a structural premium.",
            "readOnly": true,
            "wrap": true
          },
          "A4": {
            "value": "",
            "readOnly": true
          },
          "A5": {
            "value": "Defensible Long-Run Anchor",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A6": {
            "value": "Global mature peer comps at 20x EV/EBITDA represents the more defensible long-run anchor because: (i) it reflects stable-state decorative paints economics (mid-single-digit growth, normalized margins), (ii) both Sherwin-Williams and AkzoNobel NV have weathered sector cycles and prove sustainable 15–20x valuations even at premium returns (ROCE 15–35%), and (iii) Indian peer multiples (28x) remain vulnerable to cyclical compression as growth moderates post-FY27.",
            "readOnly": true,
            "wrap": true
          },
          "A7": {
            "value": "",
            "readOnly": true
          },
          "A8": {
            "value": "Why Both Peer Sets Matter",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A9": {
            "value": "Indian peers answer 'what is Asian Paints worth in today's high-growth, premium environment?' Global peers answer 'what is it worth at steady-state?' The gap between them (₹2,400 vs ₹1,400) is not analytical error—it is the valuation of growth and margin resilience. Reconciling this gap requires an explicit conviction on whether 12% growth at 22.5% EBITDA margin is repeatable or mean-reverting.",
            "readOnly": true,
            "wrap": true
          },
          "A10": {
            "value": "",
            "readOnly": true
          },
          "A11": {
            "value": "Recommendation & Range",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A12": {
            "value": "Fair value range: ₹1,400–₹2,400/share (global mature to Indian peer comps). Implied target (blended midpoint ~₹1,900) suggests 35% downside to market price of ₹2,920. Recommendation: SELL / REDUCE. Structural support comes from three independent anchors (DCF, global comps, even Indian comps as upside case) all indicating asymmetric downside. The gap to market reflects either (i) market mispricing of long-term growth sustainability, or (ii) our methodology's inability to capture intangible brand/moat value. Given the magnitude of the gap and consistency of the signal, the risk/reward tilts downward.",
            "readOnly": true,
            "wrap": true
          }
        }
